---
layout: article
title: Metrics
description: How Appwrite Analytics calculates visitors, visits, pageviews, bounce rate, visit duration, and engagement time, which breakdowns it offers, and how to read metrics from your own code.
---

Every figure on an analytics dashboard comes from the events your property receives. This page defines each metric and breakdown, and shows how to read the same numbers from your own code.

# Dashboard metrics {% #dashboard-metrics %}

The row of metrics at the top of the dashboard covers the selected date range.

| Metric | What it measures |
| --- | --- |
| **Unique visitors** | Distinct visitors in the range. Someone who returns several times counts once. |
| **Visits** | Visits with at least one event in the range. |
| **Pageviews** | `pageview` events, including repeat views of the same page. |
| **Views per visit** | Pageviews divided by visits. |
| **Bounce rate** | The share of visits with fewer than two pageviews. |
| **Visit duration** | The average time from a visit's first event to its last. A visit with a single event counts as 0 seconds. |
| **Engagement time** | The average active time per visit, counting only visits that reported any. On the web, a page is active while it's visible. In a Flutter app, the app is active while it's in the foreground. |

The PDF report and the API also include **Scroll depth**, the average of the deepest scroll positions that pages reported, from `0` to `100` percent.

These metrics include bot traffic. The **Humans vs bots** card shows how much of it comes from bots. For how Appwrite identifies visitors and splits their events into visits, see [Visitors and sessions](/docs/products/analytics/visitors).

# Breakdowns {% #breakdowns %}

The cards below the chart break traffic down by one dimension at a time and rank the values by visitors. The API uses the same dimensions, by the names in this table.

| Dimension | Dashboard card | Values |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `channel` | Traffic sources | `direct`, `search`, `social`, `ai`, `email`, `paid`, or `referral` |
| `referrerSource` | Traffic sources | The referring hostname, such as `google.com` |
| `utmSource`, `utmMedium`, `utmCampaign`, `utmContent`, `utmTerm` | Traffic sources | UTM parameters from the URL a visitor landed on |
| `page` | Pages | The page path, such as `/pricing` |
| `entryPage`, `exitPage` | Pages | The first and last page of each visit |
| `hostname` | Pages | The hostname of the page |
| `country` | Locations | Two-letter country code, such as `US` |
| `region`, `city` | Locations | Region and city names, such as `California` |
| `browser` | Technology | Such as `Chrome`, `Mobile Safari`, or `Firefox` |
| `operatingSystem` | Technology | Such as `Windows`, `iOS`, `Mac`, or `Android` |
| `device` | Technology | `desktop`, `smartphone`, or `tablet` |
| `screenSize` | Technology | `mobile`, `tablet`, `laptop`, or `desktop` |
| `trafficType` | Humans vs bots | `human` or `bot` |
| `botCategory`, `botName` | Humans vs bots, AI agents and crawlers | Such as `ai crawler` and `GPTBot` |
| `eventName` | Custom events | The event name |

Each row has the number of visitors, sessions, and events with that value. For how sources and channels are worked out, see [Traffic sources](/docs/products/analytics/sources). For bot categories, see [Bots and AI traffic](/docs/products/analytics/bots).

Screen sizes come from the viewport width: `mobile` below 576 pixels, `tablet` from 576 to 991, `laptop` from 992 to 1439, and `desktop` from 1440 up.

# Read metrics from your code {% #api %}

Read metrics with `listMetrics` and an API key that has the `analytics.read` scope. To create one, follow the steps in [Track events from your server](/docs/products/analytics/server-side#create-an-api-key) and select `analytics.read` instead.

This example lists the five countries with the most visitors over the last seven days.

{% multicode %}
```server-go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "log"

    "github.com/appwrite/sdk-for-go/v7/appwrite"
)

func main() {
    client := appwrite.NewClient(
        appwrite.WithEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1"),
        appwrite.WithProject("<PROJECT_ID>"),
        appwrite.WithKey("<API_KEY>"),
    )

    analytics := appwrite.NewAnalytics(client)

    countries, err := analytics.ListMetrics(
        "<PROPERTY_ID>",
        analytics.WithListMetricsDimensions([]string{"country"}),
        analytics.WithListMetricsDateRange("7d"),
        analytics.WithListMetricsLimit(5),
    )
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }

    for _, row := range countries.Metrics {
        fmt.Println(*row.Value, row.Visitors)
    }
}
```
```curl
curl -G 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/analytics/properties/<PROPERTY_ID>/metrics' \
    -H 'X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>' \
    -H 'X-Appwrite-Key: <API_KEY>' \
    --data-urlencode 'dimensions[]=country' \
    --data-urlencode 'dateRange=7d' \
    --data-urlencode 'limit=5'
```
{% /multicode %}

## Response shapes {% #shapes %}

`listMetrics` returns one of three shapes, depending on whether you pass `dimensions` or `interval`. You can't pass both.

| You pass | You get | Fields |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Neither | One row with totals for the whole range | Every metric on this page |
| `dimensions` | One row per value, ranked by visitors, with `value` set | `visitors`, `sessions`, and `events` |
| `interval` | One row per time bucket, oldest first, with `date` set | `visitors`, `sessions`, and `events` |

Without `dimensions` or `interval`, the single row looks like this:

```json
{
    "total": 1,
    "metrics": [
        {
            "value": null,
            "date": null,
            "visitors": 14212,
            "sessions": 22603,
            "pageviews": 63207,
            "events": 144886,
            "visits": 22601,
            "bounceRate": 46.9,
            "visitDuration": 77.01,
            "viewsPerVisit": 2.79,
            "scrollDepth": 61.3,
            "engagementTime": 75
        }
    ]
}
```

`visitDuration` and `engagementTime` are in seconds. `bounceRate` and `scrollDepth` are percentages.

## Parameters {% #parameters %}

| Parameter | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `dimensions` | One dimension to break down by, from the [breakdowns](#breakdowns) table. |
| `interval` | The bucket size for a time series: `1h`, `1d`, `1w`, or `1m`. Buckets start in UTC. |
| `dateRange` | The length of the range in days, such as `7d`. Defaults to `30d`, ending now. |
| `startAt`, `endAt` | Exact bounds in ISO 8601. Times without an offset are UTC. They override `dateRange`. |
| `queries` | Up to 10 filters. See [Filter metrics](#filters). |
| `limit` | The number of rows a breakdown returns, from 1 to 500. Defaults to 100. |

A range never starts before your plan's [data retention](/docs/products/analytics/limits#retention).

## Filter metrics {% #filters %}

Filters narrow the events every metric is calculated from. Each filter is a query on a dimension, using `equal`, `notEqual`, `contains`, `startsWith`, or `endsWith`. This example returns daily visitors who arrived from search engines over the last two weeks.

{% multicode %}
```server-go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "log"

    "github.com/appwrite/sdk-for-go/v7/appwrite"
    "github.com/appwrite/sdk-for-go/v7/query"
)

func main() {
    client := appwrite.NewClient(
        appwrite.WithEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1"),
        appwrite.WithProject("<PROJECT_ID>"),
        appwrite.WithKey("<API_KEY>"),
    )

    analytics := appwrite.NewAnalytics(client)

    days, err := analytics.ListMetrics(
        "<PROPERTY_ID>",
        analytics.WithListMetricsInterval("1d"),
        analytics.WithListMetricsDateRange("14d"),
        analytics.WithListMetricsQueries([]string{
            query.Equal("channel", "search"),
        }),
    )
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }

    for _, row := range days.Metrics {
        fmt.Println(*row.Date, row.Visitors)
    }
}
```
```curl
curl -G 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/analytics/properties/<PROPERTY_ID>/metrics' \
    -H 'X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>' \
    -H 'X-Appwrite-Key: <API_KEY>' \
    --data-urlencode 'interval=1d' \
    --data-urlencode 'dateRange=14d' \
    --data-urlencode 'queries[]={"method":"equal","attribute":"channel","values":["search"]}'
```
{% /multicode %}

You can filter on every dimension except `entryPage`, `exitPage`, and `trafficType`. Filters on `page` and `eventName` work with `interval`, or with a breakdown on any dimension other than `entryPage` and `exitPage`. Totals don't accept them, because totals also cover whole visits.
